{"parentUuid":"69a20de3-2b57-4657-914f-d1087895c833","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a38ef37448d2d0a8d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018NFJsEPbDPTrFfap6DaDJh","type":"tool_result","content":"# CP-SAT Worker Communication Analysis\n\nBased on the provided content, here's what can be determined about CP-SAT's parallel worker architecture:\n\n## Information Sharing Model\n\nThe document indicates CP-SAT uses a portfolio approach where \"diverse algorithms and strategies\" operate \"largely independently but engage in information exchange, sharing progress when better solutions emerge or tighter bounds become available.\"\n\n## Specific Shared Elements\n\nThe text explicitly mentions workers share:\n- **Solutions**: \"better solutions emerge\" are communicated between workers\n- **Bounds**: \"tighter bounds become available\" are shared across the parallel solvers\n\n## Worker Independence\n\nThe architecture emphasizes that workers \"operate largely independently,\" suggesting they are portfolio-based rather than tightly coupled. However, the document does not provide specific technical details about:\n\n- Whether learned clauses are explicitly shared between SAT-solving workers\n- The mechanism of information exchange (in-process vs. cross-process)\n- Synchronization protocols or frequency of updates\n- How bound/solution sharing influences individual worker behavior\n\n## Limitation\n\nThe provided content lacks sufficient technical depth to definitively answer whether clause learning is shared across workers or describe the implementation details of the communication infrastructure."}]},"uuid":"f6c6abbe-b51b-42fe-aba2-dbbab8ece7d3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:58.855Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"69a20de3-2b57-4657-914f-d1087895c833","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBr